STEP 1 — Connect Ethernet

Connect the Ethernet cable directly between:

Raspberry Pi (PC1) ↔ Windows PC (PC2)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 2 — Configure PC2 (Windows)

On PC2:

Press Windows + R
Type:
ncpa.cpl
Press Enter.
Find Ethernet.
Right-click → Properties.
Select Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4).
Click Properties.
Select Use the following IP address.

Enter:

IP address:       192.168.10.2
Subnet mask:      255.255.255.0
Default gateway:  leave blank

Leave DNS blank.

Click OK → Close.

Remember

192.168.10.2 belongs to PC2.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — Configure PC1 (Raspberry Pi)

On the Raspberry Pi, open Terminal.

First check the Ethernet interface:

ip addr show eth0

If you see an existing IP, tell me what it says.

If you want to directly configure the Pi with the required address, we want:

IP address:       192.168.10.1
Subnet mask:      255.255.255.0
Default gateway:  none

For now, don't change anything blindly. Run:

ip addr show eth0

and check that the Ethernet interface is eth0.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — Test the connection

Once PC1 is 192.168.10.1 and PC2 is 192.168.10.2, go to the Pi terminal and run:

ping 192.168.10.2

You should see something like:

64 bytes from 192.168.10.2
64 bytes from 192.168.10.2
64 bytes from 192.168.10.2

That means:

Pi ↔ PC2 communication is working. ✅

Stop pinging with:

Ctrl + C
